WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.695 --> 00:00:06.070
ANNIE MA-WEAVER:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:09.828
I'm Annie Ma-Weaver, a partner
manager with Google Cloud.

00:00:09.828 --> 00:00:12.370
SHAN KULANDAIVEL: And I'm Shan
Kulandaivel, a product manager

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.430
with Google Cloud,
coming to you live

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.460
from our homes and basement.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.570
And thanks for taking
the time to join us today

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:19.680
for this special event.

00:00:21.823 --> 00:00:23.240
ANNIE MA-WEAVER:
We've got content

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.430
organized in three sections.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.360
One, why Google Cloud for High
Performance Computing or HPC?

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.140
Two, some of our product
capabilities that support HPC.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.900
And three, highlighting
some of our awesome partners

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.580
that work with us to make
our customers successful.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:43.110
If there's one thing that we
want you to walk away with,

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.190
it's a Google Cloud
democratizes HPC.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.860
And we want to enable
HPC the Google way,

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.900
through our partnerships
and product portfolio.

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.350
Let's start with HPC
for Google Cloud.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:00.100
We want to share some real world
examples of HPC in action--

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.710
and how GCP is contributing.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:05.800
One of the topics that is top
of mind for us, and for many

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.160
of us, is COVID-19 and
the scientific research

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:11.922
being performed to
develop diagnostic tools

00:01:11.922 --> 00:01:12.880
and a possible vaccine.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.480
A number of different
scientific efforts are underway.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.970
There are teams working
on discovering new drugs

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:24.560
for treating COVID-19 patients.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:27.700
Others are focused on genomics,
developing better techniques

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.500
through genetic screening.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.410
There are teams that are running
molecular simulations to better

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:36.130
understand how the virus impacts
human cells and the effects

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.590
various treatments can have.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.620
All of these problems
are HPC problems,

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:45.490
where you have to perform
large numbers of computations.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.700
And in some, there is also a
large amount of data involved,

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.940
making HPC even more critical
for completing these workloads

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.770
in a timely manner.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.380
Google has joined hands
with industry partners

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.010
to create the COVID-19
HPC consortium.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:03.880
This consortium has gathered
now more than 60 projects,

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.300
with hundreds of petaflops,
millions of CPU cores,

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.950
and tens of thousands of GPUs
dedicated to COVID-19 research.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.010
In some sense, applying HPC
to such research problems

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.360
is not new.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:20.350
However, what is
new is that this

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:23.080
is an unprecedented
situation, one

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.860
that requires rapid response
and unfettered collaboration

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:30.880
across research groups, drawn
from academia and industry.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.760
Depending on the scenarios,
researchers need access

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.470
to new technologies--

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.877
in some cases, accelerators
like GPUs and TPUs--

00:02:38.877 --> 00:02:40.960
so that they can run their
simulations and machine

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.150
learning models faster.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:47.030
In other cases, they need
access to new software.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:50.350
Last but not least, given the
experimentation driven nature

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:54.370
of the research, users need
on-demand access to resources,

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:55.960
since they don't
know which project

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.670
is going to lead to success.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.190
And they need the ability
to adeptly allocate

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:01.720
resources as needed.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.580
How do you overcome
these challenges?

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.440
We believe cloud
computing in general,

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.160
and Google Cloud, specifically,
has solutions and tools

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.870
to address them.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.910
Google Cloud is
playing a small part,

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:19.910
and I want to emphasize that
Google Cloud's contribution

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.250
pales in comparison
to the efforts

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:25.550
that researchers and other
individuals are putting in.

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:28.910
Nonetheless, Google Cloud
is helping where we can.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.865
Let me tell you more about
a specific COVID-19 project,

00:03:31.865 --> 00:03:32.990
where we're playing a part.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.470
One team at Harvard
Medical School

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:42.040
is screening ultra-large
libraries of chemical compounds

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.920
to accelerate the drug
discovery process.

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:47.350
Using a grant of
Google Research credits

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.840
for the first protein
target of interest,

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.590
the team at Harvard built on
the scalable processing of GCP

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:57.360
and Slurm, a scheduler that
Google Cloud supports, to run

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.490
80,000 cores at the same time--

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:05.300
and to analyze more than one
billion compounds in five days.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:09.140
Using GCP and Google credits
to scale up its computations,

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.770
the team is able to conduct
ultra-large virtual screenings

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:15.500
much faster than ever before.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:20.149
As of May 2020, the team
has targeted 16 proteins

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:24.560
with 40 target sites and run
35 billion docking instances,

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.370
using 750 million CPU hours.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:30.530
Next, my teammate
Shan will share

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:33.080
how Google Cloud helps
other organizations facing

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.827
similar HPC challenges.

00:04:34.827 --> 00:04:36.410
SHAN KULANDAIVEL:
You may be wondering

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.660
how relevant these
challenges that Annie talked

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.270
about in the context
of COVID research

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:45.033
are relevant to you, your
user, and organizations.

00:04:45.033 --> 00:04:46.700
Now, whether you're
a large organization

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.480
with a significant HPC budget,
or a smaller startup that

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.550
is just starting
up, there are always

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:54.710
projects where
you and your users

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:56.840
need to collaborate better.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.220
There are going
to be cases where

00:04:58.220 --> 00:05:00.230
you want to experiment
with new technologies,

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:02.850
so that you can bring new
products to market much,

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.340
much faster.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:07.820
And lastly, you always want
to have the flexibility

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:11.990
and ability to respond faster
to changing business needs--

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:13.610
and do not want
to be constrained

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:16.040
by typical procurement cycles.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:19.060
In short, you want to be agile.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.410
What I see, working with
many, many customers,

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:25.190
is that many of these challenges
are even more pronounced

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.370
for customers from commercial
and industrial backgrounds,

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:31.100
more so than research
institutions.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:33.890
That is primarily because the
need for security controls

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:37.280
is higher, and typical
procurement policies are not

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.950
designed with agility in mind.

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.440
Just as Google Cloud is able
to address these challenges

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:45.860
for COVID researchers, it can
help customers in mainstream

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.390
industries as well.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:50.220
Let's look at some specifics.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.320
One of the biggest
challenges customers face

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.690
is the [INAUDIBLE]
of the infrastructure

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:57.100
that you deploy on premises.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.940
The chart on this slide
is an illustration

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:03.690
of the business impact of
having a fixed infrastructure.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:06.030
When you have infrastructure
with fixed capacity,

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.760
that are going to be times
when the infrastructure is

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.350
underutilized.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.390
And more importantly,
there are going

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:14.310
to be times when the
infrastructure is

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.790
oversubscribed, which
means some of your users

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:22.080
are going to face extended wait
times, which translates either

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.330
into delayed products
or the inability

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:28.020
to respond quickly
to new market needs.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.240
This is all in addition
to the challenge of having

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.330
an infrastructure that
can quickly go out of date

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:36.487
and not meet the
needs of your users.

00:06:36.487 --> 00:06:38.070
We talked about how
HPC infrastructure

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:41.730
is deployed on premises
and some of the challenges.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.320
With Google Cloud,
you can address

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.040
all of those challenges.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:48.150
First and foremost, you have
access to elastic compute

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:52.500
and storage, which means you get
to provision the resources that

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.020
are the right size
for your application,

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.420
nothing more and nothing less.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:00.870
Second, you also get
access to new technologies,

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:04.630
be it new software technologies
or new hardware platforms.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:06.660
A group in your
organization may want

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:10.260
to deploy GPUs for a new
algorithm or a new application

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:11.610
that they are developing.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:14.970
And with Cloud, you are able to
do that in an on-demand manner,

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:17.610
where as in on-premises, you
have to wait multiple weeks

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:20.190
or multiple months
to deploy GPUs--

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:22.050
and then figure out
who is going to go

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.600
manage that infrastructure.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.510
Lastly, Google Cloud
also makes data

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:30.150
sharing with internal and
external collaborators

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.130
easy and secure.

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:34.440
By having all of
your data centrally

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:38.520
collected and curated, you
can effectively and securely

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.880
share it with users that
need to have access to it.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.580
But also you retain control.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:46.140
You are able to be
deprovision access when

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:49.610
a user or a group of users don't
need access to data any more,

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:51.450
or you can very quickly
provision access

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:55.700
when new users join
your organization.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:57.860
A number of customers
today use Google Cloud

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.900
for those very same reasons.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.620
Customers across industries,
ranging from life sciences

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:07.640
to financial services to chip
design to weather modeling,

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:10.250
all use Google Cloud today.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:13.640
That is in addition to a
number of research institutions

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:17.240
that use Google Cloud today and
get the benefits of a globally

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:22.580
available, secure, scalable,
and on-demand infrastructure.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.400
You can find details about
these and other customers

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:30.530
at cloud.google.com/customers.

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:33.539
With that, let me pivot
and talk a little bit

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:35.850
about some of the
products and services that

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:40.080
are available to
customers on our platform.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:42.830
The most critical aspect
for HPC applications

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:46.190
is having a highly reliable
and scalable infrastructure

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.590
that is delivered as
an on-demand service.

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.110
To that end, Google
Cloud's infrastructure

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:54.470
as a service product,
spanned the breadth and depth

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:56.830
of what users need.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:59.020
Compute Engine, which
is the virtual machines

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:01.750
product in Google Cloud,
provides flexible size

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:05.920
virtual machines that start up
in seconds while also allowing

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:09.130
for custom configuration
of processor, memory,

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.470
and persistent
storage to best suit

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:13.660
the needs of your applications.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:16.690
Compute Engine VMs also
support Linux and Windows

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:18.910
operating systems,
thereby allowing

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:22.510
you to move applications that
need both Linux and Windows

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:25.500
operating systems
to Google Cloud.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:29.370
You have the flexibility to
choose between Intel, and AMD

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:30.720
processors.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:33.360
You also get a
choice of processors

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:35.400
that are optimized
for high clock

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.950
speed and vertically
scaling applications,

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:41.842
versus those that are optimized
for scale out applications.

00:09:41.842 --> 00:09:43.800
There are a number of
sessions in the Compute--

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:46.840
[AUDIO OUT] the details of the
Compute Engine virtual machines

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:51.180
product, I highly recommend
watching some of those.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:54.330
Engine is backed by Google
Cloud's high performance

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:56.930
software defined network.

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:58.516
The computer
networking [AUDIO OUT]

00:09:58.516 --> 00:10:03.180
VMs between themselves, as well
as [AUDIO OUT] and the internet

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:06.610
with high throughput,
low latency networking.

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:10.690
Similarly, Google Cloud offers
an array of storage products,

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:14.390
ranging from object storage
to file based storage.

00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:16.390
We'll cover some of the
details of the storage

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:18.730
and networking products
in the subsequent slides.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:24.730
This one specific
virtual machine type

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:26.260
that I want to highlight--

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:30.590
a new product that we launched
last year, C2 [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:32.980
for Compute Optimized
Virtual Machines

00:10:32.980 --> 00:10:37.850
is a new VM type that is
optimized for HPC applications.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:41.740
It provides 3.8 gigahertz
of sustained clock speed.

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:44.680
It provides full transparency
into the underlying server

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:49.670
architecture, thereby enabling
advance performance tuning.

00:10:49.670 --> 00:10:53.020
It also supports up to
three terabytes of local SSD

00:10:53.020 --> 00:10:56.500
for high speed ephemeral
storage and provides

00:10:56.500 --> 00:11:00.392
32 Gbps of network throughput.

00:11:00.392 --> 00:11:03.670
C2 VMs also include a
number of software features

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:07.090
that HPC applications
can take advantage of.

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:10.450
For example, it supports
new mobile applications,

00:11:10.450 --> 00:11:12.820
which enables applications
to further improve

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:18.160
their performance by
reducing memory access times.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:20.540
In order to better
support HPC workloads,

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:23.350
we also launched a capability
that gives customers

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.930
control over the
physical placement

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:27.740
of their virtual machines.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:30.940
When it comes to inter-node
latency in a HPC cluster,

00:11:30.940 --> 00:11:33.560
[INAUDIBLE] come into play.

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:36.238
The further apart your
nodes are, the more time

00:11:36.238 --> 00:11:37.780
it is going to take
for packets to be

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.790
exchanged between the
nodes in your cluster.

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:43.180
There are algorithms,
often referred to as IT

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:47.770
couple algorithms, that are
very sensitive to this latency.

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:50.430
As a result, we now
give you the ability

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:53.250
to set a policy that
insulates our placement

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:57.170
algorithms to place the virtual
machines as close as possible.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:59.010
And in most cases,
that is going to be

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:01.560
within the same physical
data center rack,

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.210
so that you get the lowest
possible latency possible.

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:10.900
One of the reasons customers
want to move to cloud

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:12.930
is so that they can
optimize their spend

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:16.320
and get the most value
for their cloud spend.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:19.380
There are quite a few mechanisms
available in Google Cloud that

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.660
allows you to optimize
your TCO, but I

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:24.630
want to highlight a couple
that really speak to the way

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:28.260
that Google Cloud in general has
been architected and engineered

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:30.860
from ground up to give
you the flexibility.

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:33.210
The first, our custom
machine types--

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.430
the ability to create
virtual machines

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:39.360
with the amount of CPU
and memory that you need.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:42.770
While you may be familiar with
predefined virtual machines

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:44.730
that you may have used
in a different cloud

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:47.850
environment, which by the way,
is optimized for provider's

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.360
efficiency rather than
customers' TCO, in Google

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.550
Cloud by adjusting
a slider, you can

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:56.573
choose the amount of CPU
and memory that you need.

00:12:56.573 --> 00:12:58.990
And Google Cloud is the only
place where that is possible.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:05.060
Next, Google Cloud has a feature
called Sustained Use Discounts

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:07.880
that automatically gives
you bigger discounts

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:10.220
the more you use your
virtual machines.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:13.340
There is no custom policy that
you need to set as a user.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:14.570
There is no subscription.

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.300
There is no special flag
that you need to activate.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:20.150
Just use your virtual machines,
and the more you use it,

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:22.160
you get a bigger discount.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:24.200
These two capabilities
are in addition

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:25.610
to some of the
other capabilities

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:27.080
that you see
elsewhere, as well--

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:29.720
Committed Use Discounts, which
gives you a bigger discount

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:31.980
if you commit for a
longer duration of time,

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:34.610
Preemptible VMs, which
gives you up to 80%

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:36.980
of discount, which is
great for workloads

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:38.105
that can tolerate failures.

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:43.400
CPUs are great for a
broad set of workloads.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.230
That's why we all use CPUs.

00:13:45.230 --> 00:13:47.300
However, there are
algorithms that

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:50.330
benefit from the massive
amount of parallelism

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:54.050
that accelerators,
such as GPUs, offer.

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:58.340
On Google Cloud, in addition
to offering AMD and Intel CPUs,

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:01.700
we provide in in-media GPUs
that Annie will talk about

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:02.840
in a little bit--

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:06.140
and the custom design and
built ASA for machine learning,

00:14:06.140 --> 00:14:08.270
called TPUs.

00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:12.440
On the one hand, GPUs enable
graphics visualization

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:15.800
and can also be used as general
purpose computation units

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:17.630
for speeding up your
algorithms, including

00:14:17.630 --> 00:14:19.520
machine learning algorithms.

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:23.230
On the other hand, DPUs, short
for Denser Processing Units,

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:24.950
are specifically
designed for machine

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:27.140
learning use cases
in mind by Google--

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:31.190
and help accelerate the time
to solution for AI and machine

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:32.661
learning applications.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:39.570
Storage services are a critical
part of cloud infrastructure

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:43.140
and play a very important
role for HPC applications.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:46.290
Google Cloud provides a
breadth of storage options,

00:14:46.290 --> 00:14:49.090
some offered from Google and
some offered from our partners,

00:14:49.090 --> 00:14:53.430
such as DDN and Netapp,
and customers can apply

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:57.120
their [INAUDIBLE] [AUDIO OUT]
to their HPC apps.

00:14:57.120 --> 00:14:59.250
They can deploy a scale
out Filestore or using

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:00.900
Cloud Filestore.

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:04.110
If they need a parsecs
compliant powerful system,

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:07.800
they can deploy Lustre file
system that is provided by DDN

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.806
and is available
in our marketplace.

00:15:10.806 --> 00:15:13.620
Cloud Storage
volumes from Netapp

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:17.890
are [AUDIO OUT] cloud
network attached storage.

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:19.890
Now in all of these
cases, customers

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:23.910
can use Google Cloud Storage
for cost efficient cold storage

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:26.610
of data, irrespective of
what storage system is

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:29.470
in use in their HPC cluster.

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:32.280
Finally, they can also use
Data Transfer tools that are

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:36.240
provided by Google to move
data from their on-premises

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.640
environment into
Google Cloud Storage--

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:41.010
and then use that
as a staging ground

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:42.765
to hydrate their HPC clusters.

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:48.320
No infrastructure
can be HPC ready

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:50.670
without strong
networking capabilities,

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:53.760
particularly for tightly
coupled applications.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:56.420
As I said earlier, the fully
software defined network

00:15:56.420 --> 00:15:59.135
that Google Cloud operates
provides all the policy

00:15:59.135 --> 00:16:02.630
driven controls that you would
expect from a modern SDN--

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:05.690
but at the same time
provides raw performance

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:07.640
that you need for your
network performance

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:09.890
sensitive applications.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:15.080
Google Cloud currently
supports up to 32 Gbps on VMs

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:19.367
and up to 100 Gbps of
throughput for GPUs.

00:16:19.367 --> 00:16:21.200
The software defined
network gives customers

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.720
control of networking policies,
such as firewall controls,

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:27.530
a global VPC, and
an architecture

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:30.800
for multi-region applications.

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:34.160
You can now run MPI
applications in Google Cloud.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:38.330
We support both open MPI
and Intel MPI applications.

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.700
And in fact, we just
recently published a set

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:43.820
of optimization
scripts and a guide

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:46.700
for tuning your application
on Google Cloud.

00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:50.990
You can find those tools in
Google Cloud's GitHub project.

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:52.940
With that, I want to
be mindful of the time

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:56.540
and hand it back over to Annie
for her to talk about Google

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:59.040
Cloud's partners and how
they help you be successful

00:16:59.040 --> 00:16:59.720
in our platform.

